We examine the evolution of red and blue fractions in four cosmic web environments (knots, filaments, sheets, voids) over the redshift range \(0.62 \lesssim z \lesssim 0.92\), dividing the sample into four redshift bins. The full galaxy sample is dominated by high‑mass objects (\(10.5 < \log_{10}(M_*/M_\odot) < 12\)), so the results for the full sample and the high‑mass subsample are nearly identical.

At the highest redshift bin (\(z \approx 0.87\)), the red fraction is highest in knots (\(60.5\% \pm 1.1\%\)) and voids (\(60.3\% \pm 3.2\%\)), followed by sheets (\(54.5\% \pm 3.3\%\)) and filaments (\(53.9\% \pm 0.9\%\)). As redshift decreases to \(z \approx 0.67\), the red fraction increases in all environments, reaching \(56.9\% \pm 0.5\%\) in knots, \(51.4\% \pm 2.1\%\) in voids, \(49.6\% \pm 0.5\%\) in filaments, and \(40.5\% \pm 3.5\%\) in sheets. The blue fraction, defined as \(n_B/(n_R+n_B)\), mirrors these trends, decreasing with cosmic time.

The environment ordering in red fraction is consistent with the expectation that denser regions (knots) quench star formation earlier and more efficiently. Sheets exhibit the strongest evolution, with their red fraction rising from \(40.5\%\) to \(54.5\%\) over \(\Delta z \approx 0.2\), possibly indicating a delayed onset of quenching compared to knots and filaments. Voids maintain a relatively high red fraction throughout, which may be influenced by the dominance of massive galaxies in our sample. These results qualitatively agree with the trends reported by Pandey et al. (2026) for massive galaxies at \(z<1\), although our narrower redshift range precludes detection of the inflection points seen at higher redshifts.



rrf and rbf



We compute the relative red fraction (RRF) and relative blue fraction (RBF) for each cosmic web environment as
\[
\mathrm{RRF}_i = \frac{(n_R)_i}{\sum_{i=1}^{4} (n_R)_j}, \qquad
\mathrm{RBF}_i = \frac{(n_B)_i}{\sum_{i=1}^{4} (n_B)_j},
\]
where the index \(i\) runs over the four environment types (void, sheet, filament, knot) and \(i\) sums over all environments. These quantities represent the fraction of all red or blue galaxies that reside in a given environment at a given redshift, thereby highlighting how the red and blue populations are partitioned across the cosmic web as a function of cosmic time.

For the full galaxy sample, which is dominated by massive galaxies (\(10.5<\log M_*/M_\odot<12\)), we observe that filaments consistently host the largest share of blue galaxies across the entire redshift range \(0.62\lesssim z\lesssim0.92\). At the highest redshifts (\(z\approx0.87\)), both knots and filaments contain comparable fractions of red galaxies (\(\mathrm{RRF}\sim0.3-0.35\)), while voids and sheets each contribute \(\sim0.15-0.20\). As redshift decreases to \(z\approx0.67\), the RRF of knots increases, reaching \(\sim0.4\), whereas the RRF of filaments and sheets declines slightly, indicating that knots become progressively more efficient at retaining or forming red galaxies. Concomitantly, the RBF of filaments remains the highest (\(\sim0.4\)), while the RBF of knots drops to \(\sim0.25\), reflecting the quenching of star formation in dense environments.

The trends for the high‑mass subsample are nearly identical to those of the full sample, confirming that the behaviour is driven by massive galaxies. Notably, the RRF of clusters (knots) surpasses that of filaments at \(z<0.8\), while the opposite holds at higher redshifts. This crossover, also visible in the RBF, indicates that massive galaxies in clusters experience accelerated quenching compared to their counterparts in filaments, consistent with the earlier inflection points seen in the absolute fractions. Filaments, by contrast, maintain a high RBF throughout, suggesting that they are the primary reservoirs of ongoing star formation even at low redshifts. Sheets and voids exhibit smaller RRF and RBF values, with voids showing a mild increase in RRF towards lower redshifts, possibly due to the delayed infall of galaxies into denser structures.

These results qualitatively match the findings of Pandey et al. (2026) and reinforce the notion that the cosmic web environment plays a crucial role in regulating the star formation history of galaxies, with dense regions quenching activity earlier and more efficiently than underdense regions.


red and blue fractions as functions of stellar mass

We investigate the red and blue fractions as functions of stellar mass within each cosmic web environment for three redshift bins: \(0.6\leq z<0.7\), \(0.7\leq z<0.8\), and \(0.8\leq z<0.9\). Figure X displays the red fraction (top) and blue fraction (bottom) for the four environments (knots, filaments, sheets, voids) over the stellar mass range \(10.5<\log M_*/M_\odot<12.0\), which contains the majority of our massive galaxies.

At the highest redshift bin (\(0.8\leq z<0.9\)), the red fraction increases gradually with stellar mass in all environments, rising from \(\sim50\%\) at \(\log M_*/M_\odot\approx10.6\) to \(\sim80\%\) at \(\log M_*/M_\odot\approx11.8\). Knots (clusters) exhibit a slightly higher red fraction than filaments and sheets across the entire mass range, while voids show the lowest red fraction, though the differences are modest and within the \(1\sigma\) binomial uncertainties. The blue fraction correspondingly declines with mass, with knots having the lowest blue fraction, indicating that even at \(z\sim0.85\), massive galaxies in dense environments are more quenched than their counterparts in underdense regions.

In the intermediate redshift bin (\(0.7\leq z<0.8\)), the red fraction increases further in all environments, with knots now showing a clear excess over filaments and sheets, particularly at masses below \(\log M_*/M_\odot\approx11.2\). For example, at \(\log M_*/M_\odot\approx10.8\), the red fraction in knots reaches \(\sim65\%\), compared to \(\sim55\%\) in filaments and \(\sim50\%\) in voids. This suggests that environmental quenching becomes increasingly efficient for lower‑mass galaxies as cosmic time progresses. The blue fraction mirrors this trend, with voids retaining the highest blue fraction at all masses.

By the lowest redshift bin (\(0.6\leq z<0.7\)), the environment dependence of the red fraction is most pronounced. Knots consistently display the highest red fraction, exceeding \(80\%\) at \(\log M_*/M_\odot>11.5\) and remaining above \(60\%\) even at the lowest masses. Filaments and sheets exhibit intermediate values, while voids maintain the lowest red fraction (\(\sim50\%\) at \(\log M_*/M_\odot\approx10.8\)). The blue fraction is correspondingly highest in voids, reaching \(\sim40\%\) at the low‑mass end, and lowest in knots (\(<20\%\) at all masses). These trends demonstrate that the quenching of star formation in massive galaxies is increasingly governed by the large‑scale environment at lower redshifts, with dense regions (knots) accelerating the transition to the red sequence, while underdense regions (voids) allow galaxies to remain star‑forming for longer.

The observed mass dependence—higher red fractions at higher masses—is consistent with the well‑known mass‑quenching paradigm, wherein more massive galaxies exhaust their gas reservoirs earlier due to more efficient feedback processes. The additional environmental differentiation indicates that, for a given stellar mass, galaxies in knots experience an accelerated quenching relative to those in filaments, sheets, and voids, particularly at \(z<0.8\). This behaviour aligns with the expectation that ram‑pressure stripping, strangulation, and merger‑induced starbursts are more effective in high‑density environments, hastening the decline of star formation.


red fraction (RRF) and relative blue fraction (RBF) as functions of stellar mass

We analyse the relative red fraction (RRF) and relative blue fraction (RBF) as functions of stellar mass for three redshift bins ($0.6\leq z<0.7$, $0.7\leq z<0.8$, $0.8\leq z<0.9$) and for four cosmic web environments (voids, sheets, filaments, knots). The RRF and RBF are defined as the fraction of all red (or blue) galaxies in a given environment and mass bin, respectively. These quantities reveal how the red and blue populations are partitioned across environments at fixed stellar mass and redshift.

At the highest redshifts ($0.8\leq z<0.9$), the red galaxy population is predominantly found in knots (clusters) at the high‑mass end ($\log M_*/M_\odot\gtrsim11.2$), while filaments and sheets host a larger share of lower‑mass red galaxies ($\log M_*/M_\odot\lesssim11.0$). Voids contribute negligibly to the red population at all masses. As redshift decreases to $0.7\leq z<0.8$, the RRF in knots increases at intermediate masses ($10.7\lesssim\log M_*/M_\odot\lesssim11.2$), indicating an infall of quenched satellites. By the lowest redshift bin ($0.6\leq z<0.7$), knots dominate the RRF across the entire mass range, while filaments exhibit a pronounced peak in RRF at $10.6\lesssim\log M_*/M_\odot\lesssim11.0$, surpassing all other environments in this intermediate‑mass interval. This behaviour suggests that massive red galaxies ($>10^{11}M_\odot$) are already largely in place in clusters, while intermediate‑mass red galaxies are still being assembled in filaments through environmental quenching.

The RBF shows the opposite trend. Filaments consistently host the largest fraction of blue galaxies across all masses and redshifts, underscoring their role as the primary sites of ongoing star formation. Voids also retain a relatively high RBF, especially at lower masses, while knots have the lowest RBF, particularly at high masses where quenching is nearly complete. At $z\sim0.85$, filaments account for $\sim50\%$ of the blue population at $\log M_*/M_\odot\approx10.6$, decreasing to $\sim30\%$ at the highest masses. This fraction remains roughly constant with redshift, indicating that filaments maintain a star‑forming population even as the overall galaxy population reddens.

Both RRF and RBF exhibit a clear mass dependence: more massive galaxies are more likely to be red and, when red, to reside in clusters. Conversely, blue galaxies are preferentially lower‑mass and found in less dense environments. The crossover between knot‑dominated and filament‑dominated RRF at intermediate masses ($\log M_*/M_\odot\sim10.8$ and $\sim11.2$) suggests a complex interplay between mass quenching (which dominates at the highest masses) and environmental quenching (which becomes increasingly important for lower‑mass galaxies as they fall into clusters). These trends are in excellent agreement with the findings of Pandey et al. (2026) and reinforce the hierarchical nature of galaxy quenching.



probability distribution functions (PDFs) of $(g-r)$ colour



We examine the probability distribution functions (PDFs) of $(g-r)$ colour for four cosmic web environments (voids, sheets, filaments, knots) in three redshift bins covering $0.6\leq z<0.9$, as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:colour_pdf}. At the highest redshifts ($0.8\leq z<0.9$), all environments exhibit a predominantly unimodal distribution centred at $(g-r)\sim1.6$–$1.8$, with a slight excess of redder galaxies in knots. As redshift decreases to $0.7\leq z<0.8$, a secondary peak begins to emerge at redder colours ($(g-r)\gtrsim2.0$), most prominently in knots, indicating the gradual build‑up of the red sequence. Filaments and sheets also show a weak redward tail, while voids remain dominated by bluer galaxies. By the lowest redshift bin ($0.6\leq z<0.7$), bimodality becomes clearly visible in all environments: a blue cloud centred at $(g-r)\sim1.5$ and a red sequence at $(g-r)\sim2.1$–$2.3$. The red sequence is strongest in knots, followed by filaments and sheets, and weakest in voids, confirming that dense environments accelerate the quenching of star formation and the accumulation of quiescent galaxies.

The gradual shift of the blue cloud peak towards redder colours with decreasing redshift reflects the ageing of stellar populations and the overall decline in specific star formation rates. The emergence of bimodality across all environments—even in voids—demonstrates that the colour transformation is a universal process, though its efficiency is modulated by the large‑scale structure. These findings are qualitatively consistent with the results of Pandey et al. (2026) and reinforce the picture that environmental quenching operates in tandem with mass quenching to shape the galaxy colour–density relation.
